/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Classification (from canonical component FACTS, never from names):
      water     declared aqueous solvent          (a_w-based VLE)
      NaCl      apparent electrolyte              (dissociatesTo Na 1, Cl 1;
                                                   its own solidPhases.halite
                                                   is the crystal admitted by
                                                   chemistryDict)
      ethanol   molecular condensable, nonionising (aqueousSpeciation none)
      nButanol  molecular condensable, nonionising (aqueousSpeciation none)

  THE MODEL, stated up front because the anchor will disagree with it:

  * Ions: Davies on water-referenced molality.  The saturated brine sits at
    I ~ 3.6 mol/kg, FAR beyond the Davies band (I ~ 0.5) -- the engine
    announces that extrapolation, and the predicted halite saturation is
    expected to sit well below Pinho & Macedo's 6.14 mol/kg.  Reported,
    never tuned: this witness measures where the composite model breaks,
    beside a primary anchor, which is exactly its job.

  * Molecular backbone: UNIFAC (water + ethanol + nButanol), the same
    group-contribution surface on BOTH liquids -- the split is an equality
    of activity, so one model prices both sides.  No fitted W-B / E-B pair
    exists in the public catalogue, and UNIFAC predicts (or here, fails to
    predict) the butanol-water split without one (the flash17 posture: an
    estimate that says so).

  * The organic phase is WET: water is a declared member (2026-08-10 --
    built against THIS case, where the dry-organic equality system has no
    root: a dry butanol liquid holds a_B ~ 0.93 while the aqueous branch
    tops out near 0.85, so no organic phase could form at all).  Water's
    cross-liquid equality carries the ionic a_w factor, and the molality
    basis is the aqueous liquid's water alone.  ONE declared exclusion
    remains, visible in the comparison: NO SALT in the organic -- the
    anchor's 0.56 wt% NaCl (ion partitioning into a low-permittivity
    solvent is different physics, the declared refusal in the builder).

  * Davies prices NEUTRAL solutes at exactly gamma = 1 (charge-only), so
    the SALTING-OUT of ethanol/butanol by the brine -- the effect Marcilla
    measured deliberately -- is invisible to the ionic leg here.  What
    moves the distribution in this model is only the backbone UNIFAC and
    the water the ions bind through a_w.  That gap is part of the finding
    this witness exists to measure.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components    ( NaCl  nButanol  ethanol  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        activityModel { ionic davies;  molecular UNIFAC; }
    }

    organic
    {
        solvent  nButanol;
        members  ( nButanol  ethanol  water );
        activityModel UNIFAC;

        reason
            "Marcilla 1995: above ~2 wt% NaCl the water-butanol split is
             present at every composition studied; the butanol-rich liquid
             is the extract phase of the published tie-triangles.";
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( water  ethanol  nButanol );
}
